{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>This is the land which I swore to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, saying, To your seed I will give it.</b> This hints to you that the Shekhinah will return in time, and this is the end of Daniel. And the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, interpreted in Berakhot, in the chapter 'One whose deceased relative lies before him': What is the meaning of לאמר ('saying')? Go and say. Rabbi Yonatan said: From where do we learn that the dead converse with one another? For it says לאמר ('saying'). The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Go and say to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: The oath I swore to you I have fulfilled for their children. And there they raised in the Gemara that the dead know — and needless to say, the pain of their own bodies, as it is written (Job 14:22): ״אך בשרו עליו יכאב״ — 'But his flesh suffers upon him' — but they even know the pain of others, and needless to say they know the affairs of that world in which they are, but even affairs in this world they know; from here that even affairs in this world they know and are involved with. And this is what they said in the Gemara: If you suppose they do not know, why did He say to them what happened? Rather, they do know; so why did He need to say it to them? — to hold Moses in good esteem. The Sages, of blessed memory, taught us here explicitly that the dead know even affairs of this world, and that Moses needed to inform them so that the Patriarchs would hold him in gratitude when he informed them of this matter, even though they already knew it themselves."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:37.531040965Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}